// Letter names (ngā ingoa o ngā pū o te arapū)
// A vowel is named by its own sound. The vowels are also words (a, e, i, o),
// so their names are given with _ and are used only when spelling.
_a	a
_e	e
_i	i
_o	O
_u	u"

// The consonants are named with a: ha, ka, ma, na, pa, ra, ta, wa
h	ha
k	ka
m	ma
n	na
p	pa
r	Ra
t	ta
w	wa

// A vowel with a macron is the vowel and "tohutō" (macron)
_ā	'a||tOhut'O:
_ē	'e||tOhut'O:
_ī	'i||tOhut'O:
_ō	'O||tOhut'O:
_ū	'u"||tOhut'O:

// Letters which Māori does not use are named as in New Zealand English
b	bi:
c	si:
d	di:
f	ef
g	dZi:
j	dZeI
l	el
q	kju":
s	es
v	vi:
x	eks
y	waI
z	zed

_cap	p'u":matu"a	// pūmatua, capital letter
_mcn	tOhut'O:	// tohutō, macron

// Names of punctuation marks and symbols (tohutuhi), said when they are read with --punct or
// spelled: Te Taura Whiri i te Reo Māori, Guidelines for Māori Language Orthography, appendix 5,
// and Te Aka Māori Dictionary. Also the names of accents (tohutio, tohurea, tongirua, tohu tiorea, tire).
_.	k'O|p|i	// kopi, full stop (Te Taura Whiri)
_,	p'i|k|O	// piko, comma
_;	k,O|p|i|p'i|k|O	// kopipiko, semicolon
